#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142610 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07e8cbe2-1fe8-5038-9b0a-061070ffe1fb" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions With Equal Denominators"}] :question/id #uuid "ae470aa4-327d-5b21-9e11-217710f1ef94" :question/math-academy-id "e-13787" :question/problem "![](images/91/916d8a6c0c4ba6c249191c0ee0d85d20f3e14ebfbabbdb0a6a5be583e39be139.png)\n\nUse the model above to find the value of $\\dfrac{5}{8} \\div \\dfrac{3}{8}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ea/ea208841c3276de2e8736add1ad5c6f4c343a9ec8d137f74f10e47b9243e87eb.png)\n\nWe can break down our division problem as follows:\n\n- Computing $\\dfrac{{\\color{red}5}}{8}\\div\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{8}$ means we want to determine how many times $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{8}$ fits into $\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{8}.$\n\n- Since both fractions have a *common denominator*, this is the *same* as determining how many times ${\\color{blue}3}$ fits into ${\\color{red}5}.$\n\n- Therefore, instead of $\\dfrac{{\\color{red}5}}{8}\\div\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{8}$, we can simply compute ${\\color{red}5}\\div {\\color{blue}3}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}5} \\div {\\color{blue}3} = \\dfrac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{8} \\div \\dfrac{3}{8} = \\dfrac{5}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142611 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fae4d19-7336-5557-8df9-bc8e0b6703fa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Missing Number"}] :question/id #uuid "0b51ee70-1d44-5427-8b9f-3930dc3a1962" :question/math-academy-id "e-13790" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c5e7367d6b3a53b513c2921d91579b1bd6588b850622ecf1c7b370695227a64d.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the equation below.\n![](images/e8/e8b1d1d44a8b8b08fa31061f8a87e632a8422f3494d77d9b6d8ba02a446c3e65.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The denominators are $5$ and $10.$ We can make a common denominator of $10$ by splitting the shape on the left into $2$ parts vertically:  \n\n![](images/3d/3d4839662bcac31bc197436b533da61e07ecf1ca301b3fa7613d433833e14028.png)\n\n \n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}6}{10} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}7}{10}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}6} \\div {\\color{blue}7} = \\dfrac{6}{7}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n![](images/58/583d7875ca78bb24742c402e222b4ba221388163d17f2056372e204d27373043.svg)\n\nHence, the missing number is $6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142612 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab62b651-0d87-5ed3-b749-11d1e25be6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions Using Models"}] :question/id #uuid "daa61f81-4882-5af9-b86a-8b03a586e93b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13792" :question/problem "![](images/87/87ce51fed33e907d6acb7e213bec596ea97b16e739bb96b5446975dc6a593011.png)\n\nCalculate $\\dfrac{2}{3} \\div \\dfrac{3}{4}$ using the model above." :question/worked-solution "The denominators are $4$ and $3.$ We can make a common denominator of $4\\times 3 = 12.$ \n\nWe split the shape on the left of the division sign into $4$ parts vertically, and we split the shape on the right into $3$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/93/931f2220f4f5b4e8d3760bcf283d19ef565d2bd8910db6d95cd44e604782a0f0.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}8}{12} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}9}{12}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}8} \\div {\\color{blue}9} = \\dfrac{8}{9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3} \\div \\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{8}{9} \\, .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142613 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50b35123-63ff-559f-b9b6-61ea6915ce0a" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions When the Result Is a Mixed Number"}] :question/id #uuid "066a9329-d63e-55d4-bc6b-bcdb5dd96cc3" :question/math-academy-id "e-13793" :question/problem "![](images/93/93a73e4b7458251fdb96891e2b00af4fd5ecf6a442aed9fb7c289ca25ae0e389.png)\n\nUse the model above to find the value of $\\dfrac{11}{14} \\div \\dfrac{3}{7}.$" :question/worked-solution "The denominators are $7$ and $14.$ We can make a common denominator of $14.$\n\nWe split the shape on the right of the division sign into $2$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/a9/a9867f3e62605ccbaad6fdc6a1925173a460f287e36b8cc563ab62bb3074a13c.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}11}{14} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}6}{14}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}11} \\div {\\color{blue}6} = 1\\,\\dfrac56.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{14} \\div \\dfrac{3}{7} = 1\\,\\dfrac56 \\, .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fae4d19-7336-5557-8df9-bc8e0b6703fa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Missing Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "5e866461-304a-5092-9672-7c84dc247b77" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186140490}] :question/id #uuid "05c4dc30-e572-5db4-9e5c-4a5cd61df427" :question/math-academy-id "q-222925" :question/problem "![](images/a5/a583013eef64a37770bae73f1b17d0df310e1e290e44f1f8a500ccd543346d51.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the equation below.\n\n| $3$ | $\\div$ | $1$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $4$ | $2$ | $2$ |  |  |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07e8cbe2-1fe8-5038-9b0a-061070ffe1fb" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions With Equal Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a4eaa37-377a-5929-824f-d428d1a42dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186142258} {:answer/id #uuid "a558181a-3e97-5894-99e2-c235d37c28a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186142259} {:answer/id #uuid "0ccfae68-63c2-55d1-91fa-6fe2f48b8975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186142260} {:answer/id #uuid "4db0e950-b0cc-5ae9-88d6-8c086cf234e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186142261} {:answer/id #uuid "57463895-e1e1-5e01-a309-3e43dcaf447e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186142262}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e99e3d66-90e7-51d9-bb59-35aae7b7a7b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186142257}] :question/id #uuid "111d2c11-f19a-5d34-ba2c-4cfd8712b8f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-61417" :question/problem "![](images/57/57382cd8235f5b141e5414492d3375178da41136f547620b1dd7acfc664b158b.png)\n\nUse the model above to find the value of $\\frac{1}{4} \\div \\frac{3}{4}$."}]}
